# Problem setup:
For the quantum $f$-divergence defined by \begin{align*}
    D^{\mathrm{std}}_f(\rho \|\sigma) =  \int_0^\infty \mathrm{tr}\bigl[ (\rho - \sigma) \frac{1}{L_\rho + s R_\sigma}(\rho-\sigma) \bigr] d\mu(s)
\end{align*}
with $\mu$ a positive measure on $(0,\infty)$ such that $\int_0^\infty \frac{1}{1+s} d\mu(s) < \infty$. We study the derivatives of $f$-divergence. Denote $\rho_t = \sigma + t(\rho - \sigma)$.

# Main problem:

Calculate $$\frac{d^2}{dt^2} D^{\mathrm{std}}_f(\rho_t \|\sigma) $$ at $t = 0.5$ with $d\mu(s) = \frac{s}{(1+s)^2} ds$, and $\rho, \sigma$ are given by
\begin{align*}
    \rho = \begin{pmatrix}
        0.5 & 0.5 \\
        0.5 & 0.5
    \end{pmatrix}, \sigma = \begin{pmatrix}
        0.8 & 0 \\
        0 & 0.2
    \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}

